## Trigger the saved Cursor Automation on every new enquiry

When the user asks to find dealers, manufacturers, or send an RFQ for any item, do **not** run the repo web app. Immediately POST the enquiry to the saved Cursor Automation webhook.

- URL: `AUTOMATION_WEBHOOK_URL` from `.env` (never commit the token; never print it)
- Header: `Authorization: Bearer <AUTOMATION_WEBHOOK_TOKEN from .env>`
- Header: `Content-Type: application/json`
- Body JSON: `{ "enquiry": "<full user text>", "company": "Krishna Trading Company", "delivery": "Bhiwadi, Rajasthan", "slackChannel": "#ktc" }`

Then tell the user the automation run was triggered and that dealer research, approval, Gmail send, and #ktc logging happen in that automation. Only do the work in this chat if the webhook call fails.

## Who to contact (do not limit to authorised manufacturers)

Use @[MCP: gmail] for all email send, draft, search, label, and reply-read actions.
Use Slack to log quotes and notify the owner. If no Slack channel is configured yet, create Gmail drafts and report that Slack still needs a channel.

NEW ENQUIRY (webhook / manual run with item details):
1. Parse brand, item, model, specifications, quantity, delivery location, and any image-derived details. Keep fields editable in your summary if anything is uncertain.
2. Research authorised dealers and manufacturers all over India. Prefer official manufacturer dealer locators and distributor directories. Treat a generic search hit as Unverified, never as Confirmed.
3. Never invent email addresses. If no public email exists, leave it blank and do not select that dealer for sending.
4. Present a shortlist table: Dealer, Email, Authorised (Confirmed / Likely / Unverified), Product relevance, Evidence, Source URL, Send?
5. Default-select only Confirmed or Likely dealers that have a real email. Unverified stays unselected.
6. Draft one personalised RFQ per selected dealer. One recipient per email. Never CC, BCC, or expose other dealers.
7. **Dealer-facing RFQs are confidential:** Never name the end customer, buyer plant/site, project, PR/RFQ numbers from the buyer’s PO, or phrases like “end use site” / “end user” / “on behalf of”. Dealers see only **Krishna Trading Company** as buyer and **delivery: Bhiwadi, Rajasthan (301019)** unless the owner explicitly asks to name another delivery city. Keep buyer BOQ context in internal notes, Slack, and `.rfq-tracking` only—not in Gmail or WhatsApp to suppliers.
8. Subject like: Requirement for <brand/model or item>. Ask for quotation, availability, delivery timeline, warranty, GST/freight, payment terms, validity, and confirmation they are authorised for this item.
9. STOP and wait for explicit owner approval of the table and email text. Do not send before approval.
10. After approval, send individually via @[MCP: gmail], max 20 emails per batch. Skip invalid or .example addresses. Record message/thread ids in memory.
11. Apply Gmail label RFQ/Sent (create it if needed).

REPLY TRACKING (hourly schedule, or when asked):
1. Using @[MCP: gmail], search recent replies to RFQ threads (subjects containing Requirement for, RFQ, quotation, or known dealer addresses from memory).
2. Skip threads already logged in memory.
3. Extract: dealer, item, price, GST, freight, availability, lead time, warranty, payment terms, validity, and a Gmail link.
4. Apply Gmail label RFQ/Quotes (create it if needed).
5. Post a Slack alert with those fields plus a short next-step note (compare quotes, request clarification, or ignore Unverified dealers). This Slack post is the quote log.
6. TWO-DAY REMINDER: If an RFQ was sent at least 2 full days ago, the thread has no dealer reply, and no reminder has been sent yet, send exactly one polite reminder as a reply on that same Gmail thread. Do not start a new thread. Do not CC anyone. Use the Krishna Trading Company signature. Then log in #ktc: dealer, item, “reminder sent”, Gmail link. Never send a second reminder on the same thread. Do not remind if the dealer already replied or declined.

Always keep memory of enquiries, selected dealers, sent thread ids, and logged quotes.
Search and include all of the following, then present a table before sending:

1. **Confirmed** — manufacturer or officially authorised dealer/distributor (official locator or OEM page).
2. **Product-listed** — the supplier’s own website explicitly lists this item/model/part number.
3. **Marketplace lead** — IndiaMART, TradeIndia, or similar listing that explicitly matches the item/model/part number.

Never invent emails. Deduplicate. Skip generic “we sell tools” pages with no matching product. Marketplace leads are not “authorised”. If a listing has no public email, show the enquiry link/phone and do not Gmail-send that row.

Always wait for explicit approval, then send one email per dealer (no CC/BCC). Cap 20 per batch. Ask every recipient to confirm genuine goods and manufacturer/channel status. After 2 days with no reply, send one reminder on the same thread only.

## Replies

When a dealer quotes a price, extract price, GST, freight, availability, lead time, warranty, payment terms, validity; log it in #ktc with a Gmail link and a next-step note.

